1
00:00:00,680 --> 00:00:03,940
If you're building an app, there's one
very simple question you need to ask

2
00:00:03,960 --> 00:00:06,880
yourself before anything
else. Who is this app for,

3
00:00:07,030 --> 00:00:09,310
and what do they actually need it for?

4
00:00:09,430 --> 00:00:13,780
That question is the heart of UX research
and is a big part of what I study

5
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:16,660
in my master's degree in
human-computer interaction.

6
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:18,440
In this video, I'm going
to start from the scratch,

7
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:23,240
from the beginning, UI, UX, and then we'll
walk it all the way up to UX research,

8
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:26,530
how it is done, where UX
research is, and all of that.

9
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:28,030
You've probably heard people

10
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:32,800
say UI/UX, like one word,
UI/UX designer, UI/UX course.

11
00:00:32,820 --> 00:00:35,590
But UI and UX are two different things.

12
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:39,000
UI stands for user interface. It
is what you can see and touch,

13
00:00:39,020 --> 00:00:42,450
the button, the colours, the
layout, the icons, the screen.

14
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:45,280
UX, on the other hand,
stands for user experience.

15
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:48,100
It is how the whole product feels to use.

16
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:50,050
Can a user find what they're looking for?

17
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:51,760
Does it make sense to the user?

18
00:00:51,780 --> 00:00:53,870
Did users get frustrated using it?

19
00:00:54,020 --> 00:00:55,830
Did the user come back to use it?

20
00:00:56,080 --> 00:01:01,080
So you can have a beautiful app with a
beautiful interface that is a nightmare

21
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:04,680
to use. In that case, you have
a great UI, but terrible UX.

22
00:01:04,950 --> 00:01:07,120
So your UI is kind of part of your UX.

23
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:08,300
The experience is

24
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:11,240
just much bigger than
what is on the screen.

25
00:01:11,310 --> 00:01:17,480
Now, what is UX? User experience or UX
covers everything about how a person

26
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:19,160
experiences a product or a service.

27
00:01:19,190 --> 00:01:22,460
The term was popularised by Don
Norman when he was at Apple.

28
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:28,033
And the way he and Jakob Nielsen describe
it is that user experience covers

29
00:01:28,053 --> 00:01:31,440
every part of a person's
interaction with a company,

30
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:35,640
its services and its products. And all
of this connects back to an idea called

31
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:37,960
human-centred design. The idea is simple.

32
00:01:37,980 --> 00:01:41,216
You can make sense of it in... I
love this phrase that I found on,

33
00:01:41,236 --> 00:01:44,700
like, a Medium article. It says that
good design doesn't start with screens,

34
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:48,790
it starts with people. So you start
from the people who use a product,

35
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,840
their needs, their abilities, their
limitations, their real lives,

36
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:56,950
and you design around them.
So now, we know what UX is.

37
00:01:57,080 --> 00:02:01,260
Now, what is UX research? UX research
is how you get to know your users.

38
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:04,730
It is the work of understanding
the people who would use a product,

39
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:07,640
what they need, where they
struggle, what they prioritise,

40
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:10,780
so that you can build the product the right
way and build it in a way that actually

41
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:11,620
works

42
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:15,220
for them. Why are we
doing all this research?

43
00:02:15,350 --> 00:02:18,160
The idea is simple. You don't
want to build the wrong thing.

44
00:02:18,290 --> 00:02:20,490
You could choose to not do it
and guess your way through,

45
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:22,240
but you don't want to
build the wrong thing.

46
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:25,580
Building is very expensive. Even with
AI, you still spend your time and your

47
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:28,300
tokens. And in some cases, you
probably still need a team.

48
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:32,980
A lot of products fail because the team
built something that maybe nobody actually

49
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,400
needs. Some other products fail
because they build the right thing,

50
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:39,410
but they build it in a way that people
just couldn't find their way around it,

51
00:02:39,540 --> 00:02:41,080
or people just couldn't figure it out.

52
00:02:41,140 --> 00:02:44,380
UX research helps to catch
both problems early on.

53
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:47,570
It is cheaper to change your idea
or change your sketch than it is to

54
00:02:47,970 --> 00:02:49,980
rebuild an app that is already live.

55
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,580
And doing UX research means that decisions
about a product come from what the

56
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:57,040
users, the real people, actually do.

57
00:02:57,440 --> 00:02:59,600
Otherwise, they just come from
a random person on the team.

58
00:02:59,970 --> 00:03:04,170
Now, who is a UX researcher? Well, I
mean, a UX researcher does UX research.

59
00:03:04,220 --> 00:03:07,800
A UX researcher works closely
with designers, product managers,

60
00:03:07,900 --> 00:03:09,800
engineers, and the people
running the business.

61
00:03:09,870 --> 00:03:13,920
Their job is to bring real
users into those conversations.

62
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:17,320
A typical week for a UX researcher
might look like planning a study,

63
00:03:17,340 --> 00:03:21,500
recruiting people to take part, running
user interviews or usability testing,

64
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:25,430
then sitting down with the notes or
the recordings to find patterns.

65
00:03:25,490 --> 00:03:29,180
And finally, sharing what they found,
which is found from these users or about

66
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:33,770
these users, with the team in a way
that actually changes what gets built.

67
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:38,210
People usually mix up who a UX researcher
is and who a UX designer is a lot.

68
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:42,270
A UX researcher's job is to
understand the users, what they need.

69
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:46,180
A UX designer's job is to take that
understanding and design the product around

70
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:50,940
it. In smaller teams, there's usually
just one person that ends up doing both

71
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:53,820
jobs. And there are also a lot of
product managers that do research as part

72
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:59,520
of their jobs as well. What you need to
dive into UX research is just genuine

73
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:01,440
curiosity about users, about people.

74
00:04:01,540 --> 00:04:04,140
You have to be really good at
taking the time to study people,

75
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:06,740
find patterns, and just be
really good at listening.

76
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:08,680
Basically, you must be
interested in people.

77
00:04:08,780 --> 00:04:10,960
When I started reading and
studying about UX research,

78
00:04:10,980 --> 00:04:13,790
I kept running into different
ways that people categorise it.

79
00:04:13,970 --> 00:04:16,360
You go on Coursera. Coursera
has quite a number.

80
00:04:16,380 --> 00:04:17,770
You go to different places. It's

81
00:04:18,360 --> 00:04:20,120
a different kind of
categorization everywhere.

82
00:04:20,140 --> 00:04:24,040
You see generative and evaluative,
qualitative and quantitative,

83
00:04:24,060 --> 00:04:27,240
behavioural and attitudinal
primary and secondary, remote,

84
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:29,240
in-person, moderated, unmoderated.

85
00:04:29,470 --> 00:04:33,440
But the useful way to think about it is
that some of these pairs describe what

86
00:04:33,460 --> 00:04:36,190
kind of information you get, like
quantitative and qualitative.

87
00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:40,640
And others describe how you run the
research, like remote or in-person.

88
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:42,560
Anyways, there's a lot of categorization.

89
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:45,700
I will focus on the first
three big pairs in this video,

90
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:48,380
generative and evaluative,
qualitative and quantitative,

91
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:53,000
behavioural and attitudinal. The first
big way to split UX research is generative

92
00:04:53,020 --> 00:04:58,150
versus evaluative. Generative UX research
happens early before anything is designed.

93
00:04:58,260 --> 00:05:00,660
It asks questions like,
what is the real problem?

94
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:03,840
What do people need? How do they get by
right now without this thing that we

95
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:07,680
are about to build? It generates
ideas for what to build.

96
00:05:07,700 --> 00:05:10,490
For example, for my thesis,
I'm doing generative research.

97
00:05:10,790 --> 00:05:14,820
Evaluative research, on the other hand,
happens later after there is a design.

98
00:05:14,840 --> 00:05:18,360
It asks questions like, does
this product actually work?

99
00:05:18,460 --> 00:05:22,260
Can users use it? Where do the
users get stuck while using it?

100
00:05:22,280 --> 00:05:24,920
So you're evaluating
something that already exists.

101
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:28,530
So generative UX research
helps you decide what to build.

102
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:32,450
And evaluative UX research
tells you whether what you build

103
00:05:32,860 --> 00:05:37,730
works. The second way to split UX research
is qualitative versus quantitative.

104
00:05:38,140 --> 00:05:40,740
Quantitative UX research is about numbers.

105
00:05:40,910 --> 00:05:42,600
How many people completed the task?

106
00:05:42,620 --> 00:05:44,540
What percentage dropped off at checkout?

107
00:05:44,560 --> 00:05:48,830
How long did it take? It tells you
what is happening and how much of it.

108
00:05:48,990 --> 00:05:51,820
Qualitative UX research
is about understanding.

109
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:54,440
Why did the users drop off?
What were they thinking?

110
00:05:54,640 --> 00:05:57,940
What confused them? It's usually
smaller and more in depth.

111
00:05:57,960 --> 00:06:00,730
It tells you the why.
Usually, you want both.

112
00:06:00,820 --> 00:06:04,560
You want the numbers to show where the
problem is, and you want the conversation

113
00:06:04,580 --> 00:06:06,860
to show why the problem is happening.

114
00:06:06,920 --> 00:06:11,220
Now, the third big way to split UX research
is behavioural versus attitudinal.

115
00:06:11,860 --> 00:06:15,820
Attitudinal UX research is about
what users say, what they think,

116
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:18,750
what they feel, what they
tell you that they do.

117
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,290
Surveys and interviews
are mostly attitudinal.

118
00:06:22,120 --> 00:06:26,000
behavioural UX research, on the other
hand, is about what people actually do.

119
00:06:26,020 --> 00:06:27,480
So you watch them use the thing.

120
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:29,470
You look at the data and what they click.

121
00:06:29,660 --> 00:06:33,530
And the reason why I want to hammer on
this, because this is one of the things

122
00:06:33,740 --> 00:06:36,530
that was so hammered into my
ears during my first semester,

123
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:41,060
that what people say and what
people do are often very different.

124
00:06:41,090 --> 00:06:44,520
What people say, what they give you in
a survey, is usually different from what

125
00:06:44,540 --> 00:06:49,020
they do when you watch them. If you
ask someone how they use an app,

126
00:06:49,040 --> 00:06:51,460
they will give you an answer.
If you watch them use the app,

127
00:06:51,480 --> 00:06:53,980
they could end up doing something
completely different from the answer that

128
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:56,900
they gave you. And they might
genuinely believe what they told you.

129
00:06:56,920 --> 00:07:00,620
We are human beings. We are just not very
good at noticing our own behaviours.

130
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:04,300
So good UX research
watches as much as it asks.

131
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:08,480
What people say still matters. Sometimes
they do exactly what they tell you.

132
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:11,000
But when what they say and
what they do don't match,

133
00:07:11,020 --> 00:07:14,880
then working out why they don't
match is also a very useful finding.

134
00:07:14,930 --> 00:07:17,150
Now, there are a few more
categories of UX research

135
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:19,120
that I'll go over very quickly.

136
00:07:19,300 --> 00:07:21,880
The primary research is the
research you do yourself,

137
00:07:21,900 --> 00:07:23,540
like running your own interview.

138
00:07:23,560 --> 00:07:27,280
Secondary research uses what already
exists, like published studies,

139
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:28,620
industry reports and all of that.

140
00:07:28,700 --> 00:07:32,180
Remote UX research happens over a
video call through an online tool.

141
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:35,240
In-person research happens in the same
room where you can notice things like

142
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:38,500
body language and all of that. And
then we have moderated research,

143
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:43,020
where a researcher is there, guiding
the session, asking follow-up questions.

144
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:46,720
And unmoderated research, where
people complete tasks on their own,

145
00:07:46,740 --> 00:07:48,730
usually through a tool that records them.

146
00:07:48,860 --> 00:07:50,460
So how is UX research actually done?

147
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:53,950
There are a lot of methods that I've
actually mentioned in the earlier part.

148
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:55,900
There's user interview, where
you sit down with people,

149
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:58,320
you talk to them about their lives,
their work, their frustration.

150
00:07:58,400 --> 00:08:00,000
Maybe how they use a
product and all of that.

151
00:08:00,020 --> 00:08:03,560
There's usability testing. You give
someone a task to do with your design.

152
00:08:03,580 --> 00:08:05,630
Maybe with your app, you
watch how they do the tasks,

153
00:08:05,860 --> 00:08:07,560
usually while they think out loud.

154
00:08:07,770 --> 00:08:10,770
Then we have surveys. You can reach
a lot of people quickly with surveys.

155
00:08:10,950 --> 00:08:12,510
But surveys usually have less depth.

156
00:08:12,860 --> 00:08:16,220
And then there's contextual inquiry,
where you go to where people work and live

157
00:08:16,240 --> 00:08:18,080
and watch them in their real environment.

158
00:08:18,130 --> 00:08:22,840
There are also diary studies, where
people record their experiences over days

159
00:08:22,860 --> 00:08:26,970
or weeks. So you see patterns that maybe
a single session would have missed.

160
00:08:27,340 --> 00:08:30,380
There's card sorting. There's A/B testing.

161
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:33,700
There's all kinds of UX research methods
that we can't go over in this video.

162
00:08:33,870 --> 00:08:37,200
You might be thinking that UX research sounds
like something that only big companies

163
00:08:37,220 --> 00:08:40,530
with big budgets can do.
But even as a small startup,

164
00:08:40,760 --> 00:08:43,290
some research is always
better than no research.

165
00:08:43,530 --> 00:08:46,510
You don't need a lab. Talk to five people
who would actually use your product

166
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:50,660
before you start to build maybe
yourself and a few friends.

167
00:08:51,140 --> 00:08:54,880
Watch three of your friends try your
app on a video call with messages,

168
00:08:54,900 --> 00:08:59,000
with feedback. That also counts, because
good design goes so much deeper than

169
00:08:59,020 --> 00:09:02,860
just a nice visual. It's about whether
the thing actually works for the people

170
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:07,580
using it. Now, another question I wanted
to answer is that is UX research the

171
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:09,980
same as user research? Most of the
time, people use these two terms.

172
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:13,470
They mean the same thing. You will
see even in job titles that use

173
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:17,040
either one. Now, user
research is the broader one.

174
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:19,200
UX research sits under it.

175
00:09:19,560 --> 00:09:22,000
User research is about
understanding the users themselves,

176
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:23,810
the users, their needs,
their habits, their lives.

177
00:09:23,900 --> 00:09:27,300
And it can feed into a lot of
decisions that go beyond design.

178
00:09:27,320 --> 00:09:31,820
For example, research into how customers
feel about your prices or your delivery

179
00:09:31,840 --> 00:09:35,570
time is user research. It isn't really
about the experience of your product.

180
00:09:36,040 --> 00:09:40,510
UX research is more focused on how people
experience a specific product or service.

181
00:09:40,530 --> 00:09:44,310
Either way, they share the same goal,
which is making sure that decisions are

182
00:09:44,330 --> 00:09:48,200
based on real users. And again, both of
them also differ from market research.

183
00:09:48,220 --> 00:09:51,240
For example, market research is
more about what people would buy.

184
00:09:51,260 --> 00:09:55,270
UX research is about how people use
the product, how it makes them feel,

185
00:09:55,340 --> 00:09:59,060
how it feels to them as well.
Will AI replace UX research?

186
00:09:59,080 --> 00:10:00,900
Very good question that
I also wanted to answer.

187
00:10:00,990 --> 00:10:03,490
AI tools are already doing
a lot of the slow work.

188
00:10:03,560 --> 00:10:04,620
They can transcribe interviews.

189
00:10:04,640 --> 00:10:06,230
They can summarise hours of notes.

190
00:10:06,430 --> 00:10:08,740
It saves UX researchers a lot of time.

191
00:10:08,950 --> 00:10:12,200
I cannot even imagine having to
do my thesis four years ago.

192
00:10:12,220 --> 00:10:14,740
I probably would do it, but oh my
God, it would be a lot of work.

193
00:10:14,900 --> 00:10:17,820
So AI is changing how UX research
gets done, in a good way,

194
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:22,410
I think, if you use it well. Now, if you're
thinking about a career in UX research,

195
00:10:22,750 --> 00:10:26,460
I'm still a student. I'm currently doing
my master's in human computer interaction.

196
00:10:26,560 --> 00:10:30,040
And from what I've noticed, the number
of jobs with the title UX researcher

197
00:10:30,060 --> 00:10:33,180
is actually small compared
to other design roles.

198
00:10:33,200 --> 00:10:35,980
I think it's because a lot of companies
expect their designers and maybe their

199
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,510
product managers to just do
some research work themselves.

200
00:10:38,760 --> 00:10:42,120
So companies have fewer people
whose whole job is research,

201
00:10:42,260 --> 00:10:43,600
but it's still a very much needed skill.

202
00:10:43,620 --> 00:10:47,020
I think it's just the entry level
that might be hard to break into.

203
00:10:47,090 --> 00:10:51,110
I wouldn't know yet, honestly. I'm not
in the job market for UX research yet,

204
00:10:51,400 --> 00:10:55,040
but that much I do know now. And
as far as to get into UX research,

205
00:10:55,060 --> 00:10:57,840
the way I'm getting into it is
by getting a master's degree,

206
00:10:57,910 --> 00:10:59,390
but I want to believe you can get into it

207
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:02,760
different ways. You can
learn the basics, the method,

208
00:11:02,780 --> 00:11:04,840
when to use one way, how to ask questions.

209
00:11:04,990 --> 00:11:08,250
There are free guides from places
like Nielsen Norman Group,

210
00:11:08,900 --> 00:11:10,520
Maze, Interaction Design Foundation.

211
00:11:10,540 --> 00:11:11,540
You want a book,

212
00:11:11,890 --> 00:11:13,400
you know, there are a
lot of books as well.

213
00:11:13,420 --> 00:11:16,840
And also try to do real research,
something that nobody's paying you for.

214
00:11:16,860 --> 00:11:19,960
Just pick an app you use, interview
a few people, watch them use it,

215
00:11:19,980 --> 00:11:22,780
write what you find, write your first
case study or something like that.

216
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:25,240
You can also volunteer to do
research with small business,

217
00:11:25,260 --> 00:11:29,400
you know, things like that. A lot of UX
researchers from what I've noticed come

218
00:11:29,420 --> 00:11:32,320
from other fields, psychology,
teaching, marketing,

219
00:11:32,340 --> 00:11:35,760
journalism, computer science. So if your
job already involves talking to people,

220
00:11:35,780 --> 00:11:38,360
figuring out what they need, maybe
this is something you might love.

221
00:11:38,380 --> 00:11:42,260
And of course, degrees going to
school, like the way I'm doing,

222
00:11:42,460 --> 00:11:46,050
